{"name":"Williams Pear Yellow","hex":"#ddc765","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"pear","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["An edible fruit produced by the pear tree, similar to an apple but typically elongated towards the stem.","A type of fruit tree (Pyrus communis)."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/pear"},{"term":"yellow","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The color of sunflower petals and lemons; the color obtained by mixing green and red light, or by subtracting blue from white light; the color evoked by light of wavelength around 580 nm; one of the three primary colors in subtractive color systems.","The middle light in a set of three traffic lights, the lighting of which indicates that drivers should stop short of the intersection if it is safe to do so."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a yellow hue.","Lacking courage."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/yellow"}],"entries":[{"title":"Williams pear","blocks":[{"text":"The Williams' bon chrétien pear, commonly called the Williams pear, or the Bartlett pear in the United States and Canada, is a cultivar of the species Pyrus communis, commonly known as the European pear. The fruit has a bell shape, considered the traditional pear shape in the west, and its green skin turns yellow upon later ripening, although red-skinned derivative varieties exist. It is considered a summer pear, not as tolerant of cold as some varieties. It is often eaten raw, but holds its shape well when baked, and is a common choice for canned or other processed pear uses."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/08/Williams_Bon_Chr%C3%A9tien_1822.png/330px-Williams_Bon_Chr%C3%A9tien_1822.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Williams_pear"},{"title":"Pear","blocks":[{"text":"Pears are a genus of trees, Pyrus, in the family Rosaceae, that bear an edible apple-like fruit of the same name. Several species of pears are cultivated for their fruit and juices, while others are grown as ornamental flowering trees."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cf/Pears.jpg/330px-Pears.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pear"},{"title":"Yellow","blocks":[{"text":"Yellow is a color between green and orange on the spectrum of light. It is evoked by light with a dominant wavelength of roughly 575–585 nm. It is a primary color in subtractive color systems, used in painting or color printing. In the RGB color model, used to create colors on television and computer screens, yellow is a secondary color made by combining red and green at equal intensity. Carotenoids give the characteristic yellow color to autumn leaves, corn, canaries, daffodils, and lemons, as well as egg yolks, buttercups, and bananas. They absorb light energy and protect plants from photo damage in some cases. Sunlight has a slight yellowish hue when the Sun is near the horizon, due to atmospheric scattering of shorter wavelengths."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d5/Cornwall_Daffodils.jpg/330px-Cornwall_Daffodils.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yellow"},{"title":"Shades of yellow","blocks":[{"text":"Varieties of the color yellow may differ in hue, chroma or lightness, or in two or three of these qualities. Variations in value are also called tints and shades, a tint being a yellow or other hue mixed with white, a shade being mixed with black. A large selection of these various colors is shown below."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9e/Color_icon_yellow.svg/330px-Color_icon_yellow.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shades_of_yellow"}],"images":[{"srcset":"htt